#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078205 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078564 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d47c0304-a438-5444-9add-5e4f61acbbcd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Index of a Term Given the First Term and Another Term"}] :question/id #uuid "426f52d9-ad0e-5ef3-a545-78315ea02c0e" :question/math-academy-id "e-1514" :question/problem "Given that a geometric sequence has the first term $a_1 = 1$ and the $4$th term $a_4 = 8$, what is the index of the term $32?$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given that $a_4=8$, we can use the formula for the $n$th term to solve for $r$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ 8 & =1\\cdot {r}^{4-1} \\\\ 8 & ={r}^{3} \\\\ {2}^{3} & ={r}^{3} \\\\ 2 & =r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n}=1\\cdot {2}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for the value of $n$ that gives $a_n=32,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =1\\cdot {2}^{n-1} \\\\ 32 & ={2}^{n-1} \\\\ {2}^{5} & ={2}^{n-1} \\\\ 5 & =n-1 \\\\ 6 & =n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, $n=6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078206 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078565 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aaa87d67-d9df-5bbb-b98e-00d72d16fe7f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Index of a Particular Term When the First Term is Unknown"}] :question/id #uuid "e3fed3e8-9bc6-558d-a426-96006871a20d" :question/math-academy-id "e-1516" :question/problem "Given that a geometric sequence has $a_2 = 24$, $a_5 = 3$ and $a_k = 12$, what is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the common ratio. We are told that $a_2=24$ and $a_5=3,$ so we can find the common ratio using a formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{n-m} & =\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}} \\\\ {r}^{5-2} & =\\frac{{a}_{5}}{{a}_{2}} \\\\ {r}^{5-2} & =\\frac{3}{24} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ {r}^{3} & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the common ratio is $r=\\dfrac{1}{2}$, we can use the same formula to solve for the value of $k$ such that $a_k=12\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{n-m} & =\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}} \\\\ {r}^{k-2} & =\\frac{{a}_{k}}{{a}_{2}} \\\\ {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{k-2} & =\\frac{12}{24} \\\\ {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{k-2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{k-2} & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{1} \\\\ k-2 & =1 \\\\ k & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078306 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078436 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90216c0e-e4a5-5933-86e0-58d218939e29" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Index of a Term Given the Recursive Formula"}] :question/id #uuid "d7c0ca60-1915-5484-913a-835a1f8fa320" :question/math-academy-id "e-4240" :question/problem "What is the index of the term $16$ for the geometric sequence below?\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = 2 a_n  \\quad a_1 = \\dfrac{1}{4},\\quad n\\geq 1\n$$" :question/worked-solution "This geometric sequence is given as a recurrence relation, i.e.,\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = {\\color{blue}r} \\cdot a_n.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\na_1 =  \\dfrac{1}{4}, \\quad {\\color{blue}r}=2.\n$$\n\nWe're interested in finding $n$ such that $a_n = 16.$ Using the formula for the $n$th term, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ 16 & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {2}^{n-1} \\\\ {2}^{4} & =\\frac{1}{{2}^{2}}\\cdot {2}^{n-1} \\\\ {2}^{6} & ={2}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the exponents gives:\n\n$$\n6 = n - 1\\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad n = 7\n$$\n\nTherefore, $16$ is the $7$th term of the sequence."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078307 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078437 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a82355f8-4b86-55c8-8686-1e4ea82e4464" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Index of a Term Given the First Term and the Common Ratio"}] :question/id #uuid "2da03f9b-c474-5b07-b4e8-bdf1dc3c2fd8" :question/math-academy-id "e-4241" :question/problem "Given that a geometric sequence has the first term $a_1 = 5$, the common ratio $r = 2$, and the $n$th term $a_n = 640$, what is the value of $n?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the index, we substitute our known values of $a_1$, $r$ and $a_n$ into the formula for the $n$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ 640 & =5\\cdot {2}^{n-1} \\\\ \\frac{640}{5} & ={2}^{n-1} \\\\ 128 & ={2}^{n-1} \\\\ {2}^{7} & ={2}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the exponents gives:\n\n$$\n7 = n - 1\\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad n = 8\n$$\n\nWe can check this result by substituting $n = 8$ into the formula for the $n$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =5\\cdot {2}^{8-1} \\\\  & =5\\cdot {2}^{7} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 128 \\\\  & =640\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557226 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078564 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d47c0304-a438-5444-9add-5e4f61acbbcd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Index of a Term Given the First Term and Another Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4897082b-ca5e-57b9-ad6b-184383f2f351" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "This term is not in the sequence" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556589} {:answer/id #uuid "5eb59004-c4ec-5ad6-84a2-38ce2cf53071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556643} {:answer/id #uuid "666704dd-2498-5e34-934f-e412a74c8bdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556671} {:answer/id #uuid "bc7efa16-d51e-54d0-8549-91ab27f2afb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556887} {:answer/id #uuid "cf614611-1cce-5ca7-b5c0-507118ac8f73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556929}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf614611-1cce-5ca7-b5c0-507118ac8f73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556929} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f96a9d2-f4e3-598d-840c-a9a1ff9c534b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557130}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2d4a048a-e911-5aea-a6de-6f7f20420447" :question/math-academy-id "q-102017" :question/problem "Given that a geometric sequence has the first term ${a}_{1}=-0.125$ and the $4$th term ${a}_{4}=-8$, what is the index of the term $-32?$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given that ${a}_{4}=-8$, we can use the formula for the $n$th term to solve for $r$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ -8 & =-0.125\\cdot {r}^{4-1} \\\\ 64 & ={r}^{3} \\\\ {4}^{3} & ={r}^{3} \\\\ 4 & =r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n}=-0.125\\cdot {4}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for the value of $n$ that gives ${a}_{n}=-32,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-32 & =-0.125\\cdot {4}^{n-1} \\\\ \\frac{-32}{-0.125} & ={4}^{n-1} \\\\ 256 & ={4}^{n-1} \\\\ {4}^{4} & ={4}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nEquating the exponents gives:\n\n$$\n4=n-1\\,⟹\\,n=5\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557291 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078436 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90216c0e-e4a5-5933-86e0-58d218939e29" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Index of a Term Given the Recursive Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34eb3836-0ad1-5d01-94f6-639a4df207d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556545} {:answer/id #uuid "3a30c082-b314-51fd-aceb-911b90ce4fd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556554} {:answer/id #uuid "a0f81088-d292-569d-84e9-5c20994e8cec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "This term is not in the sequence" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556821} {:answer/id #uuid "b260a336-b8e6-55ae-9348-9a849995f9ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556868} {:answer/id #uuid "ecd24116-ed65-5402-acc5-0096ad14fdf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557015}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b260a336-b8e6-55ae-9348-9a849995f9ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556868} :answer-field/id #uuid "6472f5d3-83a7-5401-b8c1-d5dbf474cdc2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557124}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aae39f44-86a0-57f2-8ef2-2ffdbe1ad780" :question/math-academy-id "q-102023" :question/problem "What is the index of the term $2.56$ for the geometric sequence below?\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=0.4{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=16,\\,n\\ge 1\n$$" :question/worked-solution "This geometric sequence is given as a recurrence relation, i.e.,\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=r\\cdot {a}_{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=16,\\,r=0.4.\n$$\n\nWe're interested in finding $n$ such that ${a}_{n}=2.56.$ Using the formula for the $n$th term, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ 2.56 & =16\\cdot {0.4}^{n-1} \\\\ \\frac{2.56}{16} & ={0.4}^{n-1} \\\\ 0.16 & ={0.4}^{n-1} \\\\ {0.4}^{2} & ={0.4}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the exponents gives:\n\n$$\n2=n-1\\,⟹\\,n=3\n$$\n\nTherefore, $2.56$ is the $3$rd term of the sequence."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079114 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078565 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aaa87d67-d9df-5bbb-b98e-00d72d16fe7f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Index of a Particular Term When the First Term is Unknown"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4f9efab-c17e-5931-874b-5cb792746161" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077463} {:answer/id #uuid "be4a1d02-6c11-53d8-bb2a-bbddaa5995c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077464} {:answer/id #uuid "c36b5c65-40b1-552d-862a-de2a63efc6f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077465} {:answer/id #uuid "f504c69c-388e-57d9-a607-08dc26fde645" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077466} {:answer/id #uuid "08c4a91e-aadd-53e9-b0dc-ff0807a0ad64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077467}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be4a1d02-6c11-53d8-bb2a-bbddaa5995c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077464} :answer-field/id #uuid "224c0fd0-9ee8-5e0e-b223-2902f04f8b4a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077462}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cf5edc3d-d94c-5428-950a-0bf9dfc9cd27" :question/math-academy-id "q-5418" :question/problem "Given that a positive geometric sequence has \n${a}_{3}=12$, ${a}_{5}=3$ and ${a}_{k}=48$, what is the value of $k?$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the common ratio. We are told that ${a}_{3}=12$ and ${a}_{5}=3,$ so we can find the common ratio using a formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{n-m} & =\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}} \\\\ {r}^{5-3} & =\\frac{{a}_{5}}{{a}_{3}} \\\\ {r}^{5-3} & =\\frac{3}{12} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution since we are told that the sequence is positive.\n\nNow that we know the common ratio is $r=\\frac{1}{2}$, we can use the same formula to solve for the value of $k$ such that ${a}_{k}=48:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{n-m} & =\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}} \\\\ {r}^{k-3} & =\\frac{{a}_{k}}{{a}_{3}} \\\\ {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{k-3} & =\\frac{48}{12} \\\\ {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{k-3} & =4 \\\\ {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{k-3} & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{-2} \\\\ k-3 & =-2 \\\\ k & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557239 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078564 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d47c0304-a438-5444-9add-5e4f61acbbcd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Index of a Term Given the First Term and Another Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d55bc16-8d88-5301-8c90-cb999cce4788" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556528} {:answer/id #uuid "4e988d62-a325-5411-a38e-aeb1713a5366" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556604} {:answer/id #uuid "59430c28-b313-5289-9f26-51e2fa6850bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556634} {:answer/id #uuid "8958cad0-3c05-57eb-abd5-a3866c6e2166" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "This term is not in the sequence" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556755} {:answer/id #uuid "ee8e50a0-fe53-5779-bd14-d26aff8b2c80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557018}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8958cad0-3c05-57eb-abd5-a3866c6e2166" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "This term is not in the sequence" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556755} :answer-field/id #uuid "f44da751-ee2c-53f8-8a95-946b0348206c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557203}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3e8e9f1e-2957-5c38-a228-d5c88729f321" :question/math-academy-id "q-56557" :question/problem "Given that a positive geometric sequence has the first term ${a}_{1}=2$ and the $3$rd term ${a}_{3}=8$, what is the index of the term $48?$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given that ${a}_{3}=8$, we can use the formula for the $n$th term to solve for $r$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ 8 & =2\\cdot {r}^{3-1} \\\\ 4 & ={r}^{2} \\\\ {2}^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ 2 & =r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are given that the sequence is positive (meaning all its terms must be positive numbers), the common ratio must be positive, and we discard the negative solution. Therefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =2\\cdot {2}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={2}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for the value of $n$ that gives ${a}_{n}=48,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}48 & ={2}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nHowever, $48$ is not a power of $2,$ so there is no integer solution to the equation above. Therefore, $48$ is not a term in the geometric sequence."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557277 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078437 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a82355f8-4b86-55c8-8686-1e4ea82e4464" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Index of a Term Given the First Term and the Common Ratio"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12805a45-577b-5647-8b0e-1c190396c0bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556470} {:answer/id #uuid "79e42fff-1d41-5eec-96a8-9999a8636ffb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556715} {:answer/id #uuid "862be98c-a6a3-5486-b2c5-a97ebbaf8e68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556746} {:answer/id #uuid "89fbc9df-a0b3-50f4-ba5b-92024480260b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556758} {:answer/id #uuid "a10f2412-4a5a-529c-b165-ab416e59fe52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556822}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79e42fff-1d41-5eec-96a8-9999a8636ffb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556715} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab5a5d2c-43d2-51ac-b494-a025c28a5136" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557161}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "88a280e7-5b7e-5d06-845d-b450e3c6973c" :question/math-academy-id "q-63862" :question/problem "Given that a geometric sequence has the first term ${a}_{1}=5$, the common ratio $r=2$, and the $n$th term ${a}_{n}=160$, what is the value of $n?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the index, we substitute our known values of ${a}_{1}$, $r$ and ${a}_{n}$ into the formula for the $n$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ 160 & =5\\cdot {2}^{n-1} \\\\ \\frac{160}{5} & ={2}^{n-1} \\\\ 32 & ={2}^{n-1} \\\\ {2}^{5} & ={2}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the exponents gives:\n\n$$\n5=n-1\\,⟹\\,n=6\n$$\n\nWe can check this result by substituting $n=6$ into the formula for the $n$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =5\\cdot {2}^{6-1} \\\\  & =5\\cdot {2}^{5} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 32 \\\\  & =160\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079155 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078437 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a82355f8-4b86-55c8-8686-1e4ea82e4464" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Index of a Term Given the First Term and the Common Ratio"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc1ade24-0813-534c-b204-dd86a50d71e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077709} {:answer/id #uuid "2b8dd3d2-e9e6-5004-aed0-08c248839cdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077710} {:answer/id #uuid "4a3f8614-1f2f-582e-a884-9ad2c8407551" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077711} {:answer/id #uuid "50c4a015-72b2-5006-8d21-7231a8311b65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077712} {:answer/id #uuid "52737212-edb8-5b96-81b6-926592177320" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077713}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a3f8614-1f2f-582e-a884-9ad2c8407551" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077711} :answer-field/id #uuid "c76a1c63-0dfb-50e7-8515-dceb6669108f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077708}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "56cba562-9371-5b87-a542-8607369c16b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-63920" :question/problem "Given that a geometric sequence has the first term ${a}_{1}=3$, the common ratio $r=2$, and the $n$th term ${a}_{n}=24$, what is the value of $n?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the index, we substitute our known values of ${a}_{1}$, $r$ and ${a}_{n}$ into the formula for the $n$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ 24 & =3\\cdot {2}^{n-1} \\\\ \\frac{24}{3} & ={2}^{n-1} \\\\ 8 & ={2}^{n-1} \\\\ {2}^{3} & ={2}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the exponents gives:\n\n$$\n3=n-1\\,⟹\\,n=4\n$$\n\nWe can check this result by substituting $n=4$ into the formula for the $n$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {2}^{4-1} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {2}^{3} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =24\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557242 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078436 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90216c0e-e4a5-5933-86e0-58d218939e29" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Index of a Term Given the Recursive Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d1479ac-80e3-55ec-87b3-bf22b606253a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556564} {:answer/id #uuid "4e184ef7-e071-5ed8-a113-4a5919081b98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556602} {:answer/id #uuid "8be07931-e6db-5a33-982c-96bc60dab8c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556766} {:answer/id #uuid "e8868c1f-c45d-5a44-a492-3311f4d2b9d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557004} {:answer/id #uuid "f66bb68a-94a7-50cf-80f2-a1657f726233" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557039}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8be07931-e6db-5a33-982c-96bc60dab8c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556766} :answer-field/id #uuid "e34b9672-357d-510a-9a8a-1a2e573e0c9d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557192}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4274dfd7-9570-5881-a7a2-35e55995174b" :question/math-academy-id "q-63979" :question/problem "What is the index of the term $486$ for the geometric sequence below?\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=3{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=2,\\,n\\ge 1\n$$" :question/worked-solution "This geometric sequence is given as a recurrence relation, i.e.,\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=r\\cdot {a}_{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=2,\\,r=3.\n$$\n\nWe're interested in finding $n$ such that ${a}_{n}=486.$ Using the formula for the $n$th term, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ 486 & =2\\cdot {3}^{n-1} \\\\ \\frac{486}{2} & ={3}^{n-1} \\\\ 243 & ={3}^{n-1} \\\\ {3}^{5} & ={3}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the exponents gives:\n\n$$\n5=n-1\\,⟹\\,n=6\n$$\n\nTherefore, $486$ is the $6$th term of the sequence."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557274 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078565 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aaa87d67-d9df-5bbb-b98e-00d72d16fe7f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Index of a Particular Term When the First Term is Unknown"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07fc5f99-a190-51ee-a465-407870427a03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556446} {:answer/id #uuid "0d85cf57-8864-563b-ae5e-647e27df0d77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556456} {:answer/id #uuid "32d134c8-4a8d-5f2b-af8c-f2e3bee5e218" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556538} {:answer/id #uuid "92901f09-a91d-52b4-9ebd-c14bdcccca1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556783} {:answer/id #uuid "e2e66db7-a715-5d0c-bf46-a2f73cc9a7b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556991}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2e66db7-a715-5d0c-bf46-a2f73cc9a7b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556991} :answer-field/id #uuid "e543d592-57e7-5835-bf9e-672426221161" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557194}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7b028485-bf8e-559e-a7ed-a6b72baad9fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-73407" :question/problem "Given that a geometric sequence has \n${a}_{1}=896$, ${a}_{4}=112$ and ${a}_{k}=14$, what is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the common ratio. We are told that ${a}_{1}=896$ and ${a}_{4}=112,$ so we can find the common ratio using a formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{n-m} & =\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}} \\\\ {r}^{4-1} & =\\frac{{a}_{4}}{{a}_{1}} \\\\ {r}^{4-1} & =\\frac{112}{896} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ {r}^{3} & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the common ratio is $r=\\frac{1}{2}$, we can use the same formula to solve for the value of $k$ such that ${a}_{k}=14:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{n-m} & =\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}} \\\\ {r}^{k-4} & =\\frac{{a}_{k}}{{a}_{4}} \\\\ {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{k-4} & =\\frac{14}{112} \\\\ {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{k-4} & =\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{k-4} & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3} \\\\ k-4 & =3 \\\\ k & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
